::ENG4_U06_L07_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.C.03] Publication scenario: A senior team has a one-minute narration, three slides, an infographic, a research summary, and source credits for a public-service announcement about postsecondary training. Which option best supports publication-ready multimedia communication? {
~A. Add animations before checking source credits or audience access.#Incorrect. Animations are secondary to credibility and accessibility. A presentation should first make sources, evidence, and audience access secure.
=B. Verify that narration, slides, infographic, research summary, and source credits work together for the audience.#Correct. This answer best applies Unit 6 communication skills with clear reasoning, evidence, audience awareness, and publication readiness.
~C. Publish as soon as the product contains several media parts.#Incorrect. Having several media parts is not enough. Publication readiness requires each part to support the purpose, audience, and source responsibilities.
~D. Remove evidence so the product feels shorter and more casual.#Incorrect. Shorter is not always stronger. Unit 6 communication still needs credible evidence, even when the style is concise.
}

::ENG4_U06_L07_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Publication scenario: A senior team has a one-minute narration, three slides, an infographic, a research summary, and source credits for a public-service announcement about postsecondary training. Which option best supports publication-ready multimedia communication? {
~A. Add animations before checking source credits or audience access.#Incorrect. Animations are secondary to credibility and accessibility. A presentation should first make sources, evidence, and audience access secure.
~B. Publish as soon as the product contains several media parts.#Incorrect. Having several media parts is not enough. Publication readiness requires each part to support the purpose, audience, and source responsibilities.
~C. Remove evidence so the product feels shorter and more casual.#Incorrect. Shorter is not always stronger. Unit 6 communication still needs credible evidence, even when the style is concise.
=D. Revise the product so source credits are clear and the public audience can trust the evidence.#Correct. This answer best applies Unit 6 communication skills with clear reasoning, evidence, audience awareness, and publication readiness.
}

::ENG4_U06_L07_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.C.02] Publication scenario: A senior team has a one-minute narration, three slides, an infographic, a research summary, and source credits for a public-service announcement about postsecondary training. Which option best supports publication-ready multimedia communication? {
=A. Choose the revision that improves coherence, accessibility, credibility, and professional voice.#Correct. This answer best applies Unit 6 communication skills with clear reasoning, evidence, audience awareness, and publication readiness.
~B. Add animations before checking source credits or audience access.#Incorrect. Animations are secondary to credibility and accessibility. A presentation should first make sources, evidence, and audience access secure.
~C. Publish as soon as the product contains several media parts.#Incorrect. Having several media parts is not enough. Publication readiness requires each part to support the purpose, audience, and source responsibilities.
~D. Remove evidence so the product feels shorter and more casual.#Incorrect. Shorter is not always stronger. Unit 6 communication still needs credible evidence, even when the style is concise.
}

